**1. Win32**

The Win32 API contains a way to do this via the [SetUnhandledExceptionFilter](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms680634(VS.85).aspx) function, as follows:

```
LONG myFunc(LPEXCEPTION_POINTERS p)
{
     printf("Exception!!!\n");     
     return EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_HANDLER;
}

int main()
{
     SetUnhandledExceptionFilter((LPTOP_LEVEL_EXCEPTION_FILTER)&myFunc);    
     // generate an exception !
     int x = 0;
     int y = 1/x;
     return 0;
}

```

**2. POSIX/Linux**

I usually do this via the [signal()](http://linux.die.net/man/2/signal) function and then handle the SIGSEGV signal appropriately. You can also handle the SIGTERM signal and SIGINT, but not SIGKILL (by design). You can use [strace()](http://linux.die.net/man/1/strace) to get a backtrace to see what caused the signal.

