You can use a `LoggerContext` and `LoggerConfig` to do this.

```
public class A {
    private final LoggerContext context;
    private final LoggerConfig config;

    public A(int id) {
        context = new LoggerContext("A");
        config = new LoggerConfig("A", context, Level.ERROR);
        config.addAppender(new ConsoleAppender());
        context.addLogger(config);
    }

    public void log(String message) {
        Logger logger = context.getLogger("A");
        logger.error(message);
    }
}

```

Then you can use it like this:

```
A a = new A(152);
a.log("message");
A b = new A(155);
b.log("message");

```

