I think you are mixing up the two functions. `moment()` is the moment constructor, and `moment.locale()` is the locale function. You need to call `moment()` first, and then call `locale()` on the result.

```
<script>
    var moment = moment();
    moment.locale('de');
    console.log(moment().fromNow());
</script>

```

**Update**

You can also use `moment.locale('de').fromNow()` if you want to use the locale function before the moment function. This is useful if you want to use the locale function in a different context, such as in a template.

```
<script>
    var moment = moment();
    moment.locale('de');
    console.log(moment().fromNow());
    console.log(moment().fromNow('en'));
</script>

```

